From e91f58f518c105f10fe48f50f621bcc0d61231a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claudecode <hockinglee@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:01:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] perf(crates/server): load only the clips history keeps,
 and leave images on disk

Starting the daemon read every record ever appended to the clips file and
decoded every image in the store, then handed the lot to `ClipboardManager`,
which dropped all but `max_history` of them. On a store of a few hundred images
start-up spent seconds at full CPU before the "started" notification appeared.

`Driver::load` now takes the capacity history is kept at and works in two steps:
scan the records -- image records carry only a digest, so this stays cheap --
and keep the newest `capacity` of them. Images are not read at all: a record
becomes a deferred clip carrying the digest that names its file and the size of
it, which is all that listing a clip needs. The scan reads through a `BufReader`
rather than issuing a syscall per field.

The pixels come back through the new `ImageStore` when a clip is actually pasted
or fetched. It needs no lock, because images are addressed by the digest of
their content and a stored image never changes. `ClipboardManager` holds one and
fills a clip in on `get`, `get_current_clip` and `mark`, which leaves the gRPC
and D-Bus services unchanged.

The store is also compacted while loading, which is what keeps it from growing
without bound: the clips file is rewritten to hold exactly the retained records,
and image files nothing references any more are removed. Until now that happened
only on a graceful shutdown, so a daemon that was killed left its history to
accumulate. `shrink_to` shares the routine, and both stage the rewrite in a
temporary file so an interrupted compaction cannot truncate the clips file.
Storing an image that is already in the store now skips encoding and writing it.

Note that history beyond `max_history` is discarded at start-up rather than at
shutdown; lowering `max_history` and restarting now drops the excess right away.
